1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
When the Foreign Vendor Controls the Switch. Building dependency-proof archives: a guide for Singapore records teams

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:21,833
A dependency risk is the vulnerability that exists when critical infrastructure is owned and operated by someone else, and that owner can restrict, alter or withdraw access on its own decision, not an attacker's.

3
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:28,500
A record is dependency-proof only if losing the vendor changes nothing about whether it can still be read.

4
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:37,491
On 14 October 2026, an adviser to President Zelenskyy reported an overnight, speed-based restriction on Starlink terminals used by Ukrainian forces.

5
00:00:37,491 --> 00:00:41,379
He said forces received no warning, and several missions were disrupted.

6
00:00:41,379 --> 00:00:47,157
SpaceX has given no public reason, and no independent evidence ties the change to any government directive.

7
00:00:47,157 --> 00:00:54,500
The structural point stands: the capability to change access unilaterally is itself the vulnerability. Source: Euronews, 14 October 2026

8
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:04,057
The Open Rights Group's 2026 report, Tech Giants and Giant Slayers, says reliance on a few foreign technology firms is an urgent national security issue.

9
00:01:04,057 --> 00:01:11,440
It cites legal compulsion: the US CLOUD Act and China's National Intelligence Law can require companies to hand over data or access.

10
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:20,500
It also cites sanctions enforcement reaching into services, and concentrated public-sector contracts. Source: Open Rights Group, 2026, as reported by The Register

11
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:29,370
Type of control: Access control. What the vendor can do: Throttle, restrict or cut off the service. Who is exposed: Live systems and cloud-only archives.

12
00:01:29,370 --> 00:01:37,807
Type of control: Legal control. What the vendor can do: Be compelled by its home government to hand over data. Who is exposed: Data at rest, not just live services.

13
00:01:37,807 --> 00:01:46,500
Type of control: Decision control. What the vendor can do: Let AI agents decide what is retained or surfaced. Who is exposed: Your effective command of your own archive.

14
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:55,457
Film made to ISO 18901, processed to ISO 18917 and stored to ISO 18911 carries an LE-500 rating: a 500-year life expectancy.

15
00:01:55,457 --> 00:02:05,853
It needs nothing beyond light and a lens to read. No subscription to lapse, no account to suspend, no foreign-hosted platform, no software to fall out of support.

16
00:02:05,853 --> 00:02:11,500
Digital stays the tool for daily access. Microfilm is the preservation layer underneath.

17
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:22,221
Is this only a risk for countries at war? No. The same structural risk exists in peacetime. Cyberattack versus dependency risk? A cyberattack is a break-in.

18
00:02:22,221 --> 00:02:27,642
A dependency risk needs no attack: the vendor decides. Does microfilm solve everything?

19
00:02:27,642 --> 00:02:33,500
It removes vendor control for the records on it, but does not replace working digital systems.

20
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:37,940
